<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE topic SYSTEM "https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/xhtml-entities.dtd">
<topic xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/topic.v2.xsd"
       title="Library" id="lib">

    <snippet id="install">
        <tabs group="install">
            <tab title="Command line" group-key="cli">
                <code-block lang="bash">
                    dart pub add dev:%package% dev:build_runner
                </code-block>
            </tab>
            <tab title="pubspec.yaml" group-key="pubspec">
                <code-block lang="yaml">
                    dev_dependencies:
                      build_runner: ^%min_build_runner%
                      %package%: ^%version%
                </code-block>
            </tab>
            <tab title="Flutter" group-key="flutter">
                <code-block lang="bash">
                    flutter pub add dev:%package% dev:build_runner
                </code-block>
            </tab>
        </tabs>
    </snippet>

    <snippet id="opt-in-note">
        <note>
            Every feature of %product% is opt-in. Adding the dev dependency on its own changes nothing: the builders
            are applied to your package, but they return immediately until a feature is enabled, and
            <code>dart run %package% check</code> reports that nothing is enabled.
        </note>
    </snippet>

    <snippet id="config-location">
        <tip>
            The configuration lives in the <code>%pubspec_key%:</code> section of <code>pubspec.yaml</code>, or in an
            <code>%config_file%</code> next to it with the same keys one level up. See
            <a href="Configuration-Overview.md">Where the configuration lives</a>.
        </tip>
    </snippet>

    <snippet id="trivy-required">
        <note>
            This scan runs the Trivy executable. The command line finds an installed Trivy or downloads a pinned,
            checksum-verified release; the builders need an installed one - see
            <a href="Trivy-Installation.md">Installing Trivy</a>. When Trivy is missing or fails, the scan fails with
            an explanation and exit code 69 rather than passing silently.
        </note>
    </snippet>

    <snippet id="severity-values">
        <deflist type="narrow">
            <def title="CRITICAL">Requires immediate attention.</def>
            <def title="HIGH">Should be addressed as soon as possible.</def>
            <def title="MEDIUM">Important, but not urgent.</def>
            <def title="LOW">Minimal impact.</def>
            <def title="UNKNOWN">Could not be rated. Used for licenses Trivy cannot classify.</def>
        </deflist>
        <p>Severities are case-insensitive in the configuration: <code>high</code>, <code>High</code> and
            <code>HIGH</code> are the same.</p>
    </snippet>

    <snippet id="exit-codes">
        <p>%product% follows the <code>sysexits</code> convention, so a script can tell a finding from a problem
            with the environment:</p>
        <table>
            <tr><td>Exit code</td><td>Meaning</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>0</code></td><td>Passed: no finding reached the threshold, every check that ran passed, or
                nothing was enabled.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>1</code></td><td>Findings at or above the threshold, or a failed check: unformatted files,
                diagnostics at or above <code>lint.fail_on</code>, findings in a scan that fails on findings, an API
                that differs from its dump or has none, style violations, coverage below the threshold, a changelog that is malformed or does
                not document the version of <code>pubspec.yaml</code>, a package that
                <code>inspect</code> or <code>add</code> rates at or above <code>inspect.fail_score</code>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>64</code></td><td>Usage error: an unknown command, option or scan name, a missing argument,
                a malformed <code>--set</code>, an invalid <code>--min</code>, <code>--release</code> or <code>--date</code>, a
                Git revision that cannot be resolved, a changelog command outside a Git repository, or a version that
                the changelog already documents.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>65</code></td><td>Invalid input or configuration: a broken configuration key, a named
                configuration file that does not exist, a missing or unreadable <code>pubspec.yaml</code>,
                <code>pubspec.lock</code> or <code>.dart_tool/package_config.json</code>, a changelog without the release
                that <code>changelog notes</code> asks for, a missing style header template or custom style rules
                that cannot run.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>69</code></td><td>Unavailable, so the check is incomplete: Trivy missing, crashing, or
                required but not provisionable; <code>dart format</code>, <code>dart analyze</code>,
                <code>dart fix</code> or the test run of the coverage gate failing; OSV.dev or pub.dev unreachable;
                Git missing or failing; a report or changelog that cannot be written.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>70</code></td><td>Internal error. Set <code>INSPECTRA_DEBUG=1</code> to print the stack
                trace, and report it.</td></tr>
        </table>
        <p><code>--exit-zero</code> turns <code>1</code> into <code>0</code> and never hides <code>64</code>,
            <code>65</code>, <code>69</code> or <code>70</code>.</p>
    </snippet>

</topic>
